From dbfc2ea7030a9464936e8ca1d66c0e17d04e220e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 19:47:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09307/11362] Health-check vLLM by status, not by a body only
 llama.cpp sends

The vLLM server has been coming up. Kernel v32 logged `Application startup complete`,
then served 157 consecutive `GET /health` 200s while my launcher waited out its full
20-minute budget and reported `server_up=False`.

`_healthy()` returns `b"ok" in r.read()`. That is correct for llama.cpp, which answers
`{"status":"ok"}`. vLLM answers 200 with an EMPTY body, so the check is False forever no
matter how healthy the server is.

This one is worth naming plainly rather than just fixing. The entire point of the
backend-aware pre-flight probe added four commits ago is that a llama.cpp-shaped check
reports the wrong thing about vLLM -- and a llama.cpp-shaped health check was sitting
inside the vLLM launcher the whole time I was writing that probe. Every environment fix
in this chain was real and needed, but from v28 onward this would have failed the run on
its own.

`_vllm_healthy()` checks 2xx on /health and is used at both call sites in
`_ensure_vllm_server`. Unreachable stays unhealthy, unlike the reuse check which fails
open deliberately. Pinned by a test carrying the v32 shape: 200, empty body.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw
